"""Class 10 SST Ch 19 Money and Credit - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"Money eliminates the need for a double coincidence of wants.", r"Money acts as a medium of exchange, so a seller need only find a buyer and can then use the money to buy what he wants.", "a",
     r"As an intermediate step in exchange, money removes the need to find someone who wants exactly what you offer; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Only the Reserve Bank of India issues currency notes in India.", r"The Reserve Bank of India supervises the loan activities of banks and cooperatives.", "b",
     r"Both are true, but supervising loans does not explain why only the RBI issues currency."),
 AR4(r"Demand deposits are considered as money.", r"Demand deposits can be withdrawn on demand and payments can be made through cheques against them.", "a",
     r"Because they can be used as a medium of payment through cheques, demand deposits share the features of money; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Banks keep all the deposits they receive as cash with themselves.", r"Banks use the major portion of the deposits to extend loans, keeping only a small proportion as cash.", "d",
     r"A is false. R is true: banks hold only about 5 per cent as cash and lend the rest."),
 AR4(r"Absence of collateral is a major reason why the poor find it hard to get bank loans.", r"Collateral is an asset that the lender gives to the borrower as a guarantee.", "c",
     r"A is true. R is false: collateral is an asset that the borrower owns and offers as a guarantee to the lender."),
 AR4(r"Cheap and affordable credit is crucial for a country's development.", r"Self-Help Groups help the poor get loans at reasonable interest without collateral.", "b",
     r"Both are true, but R describes one arrangement and does not explain A."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Ramesh, a shoe maker, wants to buy wheat. The farmer who grows wheat does not want shoes; he wants to buy clothes. Ramesh sells his shoes to a shopkeeper for rupees, and then buys wheat with the money from the farmer. A cheque is used when he pays the leather supplier, who deposits it in his own bank account. "
   r"(i) What is the difficulty Ramesh would face in a barter system called? (ii) Who issues currency notes in India? (iii) What is a cheque? (iv) Why are demand deposits accepted as money even though they are not currency?",
   r"(i) Double coincidence of wants (1). (ii) The Reserve Bank of India (1). (iii) A paper instructing the bank to pay a specific amount from a person's account to the person named on it (1). (iv) They can be withdrawn on demand and used to make payments by cheque without cash, so they work as a medium of exchange along with currency (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Megha has taken a loan of Rs 5 lakh from a bank to buy a house. The annual interest rate is 12 per cent and she will repay it in 10 years in monthly instalments. The bank asked for documents showing her salary and kept the papers of the house with it until the loan is repaid. "
   r"(i) What is the collateral in this case? (ii) Name any two components of the terms of credit shown here. (iii) What is the annual interest on the loan for the first year, if no repayment is made? (iv) What would the bank do if Megha failed to repay?",
   r"(i) The papers of the new house (1). (ii) Any two: interest rate, collateral and documentation, mode of repayment (1). (iii) 12 per cent of Rs 5,00,000 = Rs 60,000 (1). (iv) The bank has the right to sell the collateral (the house) to recover its loan (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A landless labourer, Rama, needs loans when there is no work. She borrows from her employer at 5 per cent per month, and repays by working for him. Arun, a farmer with seven acres, takes a bank loan at 8.5 per cent per annum. Another group of women in the village have formed a Self-Help Group of 15 to 20 members who save and lend to each other. "
   r"(i) Name the type of credit source Rama uses. (ii) Who supervises the formal sources of loans? (iii) How many members does a typical Self-Help Group have? (iv) Why is the cost of informal credit higher for the borrower?",
   r"(i) An informal source (employer or landowner) (1). (ii) The Reserve Bank of India (1). (iii) 15 to 20 (1). (iv) Informal lenders charge a much higher interest rate and nobody supervises them, so a large part of the borrower's earnings goes to repay the loan, which can cause a debt trap (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

